Numbers of this form are called *Liouville numbers*. The proof shows more generally that any real $x$ with
$ forall n in NN, exists p/q in QQ : 0 < abs(x - (p)/(q)) < (1)/(q^n) $
is transcendental: in loose terms, a real number with very good rational approximations is transcendental. The same proof does not apply to $ee$, but it is known that $ee$ is transcendental as well.
